A||||9||GT Bastard||Quick resume question!!!!!||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||09-20-2000||12:14 PM||||Is it always necessary to use a cover-letter with a resume?  This is the first time I've written one, so I'm a little lost.  I found two tech jobs that I'm qualified for, but they just tell you to send a resume to an address....  thanks!||||reg||4
Z||000001||redfiero||09-20-2000||12:36 PM||||It is not always necessary to send a cover letter.  If there is a job posted that is not your area of expertise, send a cover letter to explain why the company should give you a chance.  If the job matches your qualifications, your resume will speak for itself.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||09-20-2000||12:37 PM||||Some sort of cover letter as to the job you are applying for.  Attach the resume to the cover letter.  The resume should have the basic info name, address, education, work experience, (newest going back in time) and some job description of what you did at each job. (things like I invented the internet--need to bust Algore).  You may need to tweak your job description/work experience to better fit the job you are going for.<P>Make it believable and keep the resume short, no more than two pages.  Whomever reads these thing looks at lots of them and they need to make a snap judgment as whether or not they want to call you in for an interview.  Make sure the spelling is correct and grammer is correct!<P>Good Luck..||||reg||3
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||09-20-2000||02:38 PM||||<BR>Here is what I do, and it has always worked for me.<P>I <B>always</B> use pitch 10 or pitch 12 arial font through out my resume, because many businesses scan in received resumes and arial scans better and cleaner.<P>I <B>always</B> keep my resume to a single page.  A cover page is not, <B>NOT</B>, part of the resume itself.  Human Resource personnel go through too many resumes for you to waste their time with long detail, you want to catch their attention. When you catch their attention they will contact you.<P>I <B>always</B> give my name, address, phone number, email address centered at the top of the page.  My name is a larger font than the other information.  If you have a pager number or a cell phone number list that as well, give them as many contact points as possible, you can't have an interview, if they can't contact you.<P>I <B>always</B> give short statements regarding my work history.  Listing my responsibilities and skills, along with equipment I have experience working on.<P>I <B>always</B> list references as on request.  Don't waste space on your resume with references, if you receive an interview, they will require that you complete an application, that is where you list your references.  Always have a minimum of three (3) references, and make sure that they are not related to your via blood or marriage.<P>I <B>always</B> list my education. List the latest (whether trade school or university) education first, meaning those things accomplished after High School.<P>I <B>always</B> list any licenses or other relavent(sp) data regarding my experience and education consider for the position which I am applying.<P>I <B>NEVER</B> list my religion, sex, hieght, weight, marital status, or dependants.<P>I <B>MIGHT</B> list my hobbies, depending on available space.<P><I><B>Additional</B></I><BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.resume.com" TARGET=_blank>www.resume.com</A>  take a look around see what they say.<P>Just because you send a resume, don't assume that they will contact you.  If you want the job, you keep knocking on their door, via phone line, and keep your name in use.  Just don't go overboard to were they associate your name with bothersome.<P>Goodluck.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<P>NOTE: Edited for information I left out and mis-spellings.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 09-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||09-20-2000||03:36 PM||||In my field, we were advised to attach a good recent photo to the resume. The only responses I got back from sending out my resures were the ones with a photo, and I am currently employed at one of those contacts now, so it must have worked  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||whamel||09-20-2000||04:18 PM||||I have a cover letter with a nice eye pleasing design on the front that I had done for me by a print shop in color.  I also have listed pretty much all the things that Gold-86SE has mentioned, but I do spend a great deal of concentration on my technical certification and experience.  It seems to help me, and the 50% raise I got last time I changed jobs seemed to indicate that I have a pretty good setup.<BR>By the way, I have a brief resume that I send out as well as a more comprehensive copy I bring with me (3 copies to every interview one for me, and two for possible interviewers) in case they would like more background.  This also has copies of all my certifications in it (with the numbers removed, of course) in case they want to verify that.<BR>Wish I still knew people in Sioux Falls - Good luck on your hunt!<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||09-20-2000||04:59 PM||||Gold-86SE hit on all the do's & don'ts and good points real good.<P>Check the library, you'll find lots of good books with great examples of successful resumes for many types of jobs.  Try to find a format that fits you, your experience, and education if your not sure where to start.  Hopefully by reading over a few examples, they'll give you ideas on how you want your resume to sound, just don't copy it word for word.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  You should also find some good info on how to make cover letters in most of those books too.<P>Good Luck.<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">        <BR>        <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||09-20-2000||08:14 PM||||GoldSE pretty much wrapped it up for you there. My personal view on cover letters is if you have a contact to send it to, then yes, include a succinct cover letter.  Sometimes it is necessary to jog the HR persons memor as to who you are. If you are responding toa generic ad, not necessary and in fact only presents more opportunity for spelling or grammar errors.(Grammar errors? NEVER!)<P>As for the picture, nah, don't do it. This is an EEOC world. <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ozzy||09-21-2000||12:57 AM||||I think everyone added something worth mentioning but personally I think a resume much like redfiero's response gets the gold! Simple and to the point. And don't kiss any butt while doing it!!<P>I've hired a lot of people and usually never had any assistance from a HR dept. I did not have time to look for 'finer details'; I wanted someone who could DO THE JOB. This would be revealed to me thru the past experience they claimed to have, not thru the type of font they used...or the writing style (s) utilized in a cover letter. I like what stimpy said...IF you have a contact...<BR> Focus the attention to what you know 'cause in the end that is what gets you in, not perfect spacing between paragraphs and nice use of the Tab key!<P>I have also written about a million resumes and have agonized over these things so don't feel alone; my last resume was definatly my best for 2 reasons...1/ I stuck to the skills instead of trying to pretend I know something I dont. 2/ It just got me my dream job with total package worth 6 figures annually. I do not have a degree or formal training!<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnsFiero||09-21-2000||01:35 AM||||Don't forget to proofread, proofread, and proofread again.  Best if done by someone else as it is impossible for you to catch some of your own mistakes.  Also don't forget to triple check your spelling - nothing turns an employer off more than bad spelling.<P>John||||reg||1
